# ---------------------------------------------------------------- BATAVIA
dict(
 id='batavia', title='Batavia', dates='1629', short='Batavia',
 question='What can the records still not tell us?',
 tile_img=('img/tile-batavia.jpg','A ship silhouette drawn for the Reopen the Record apps, not a picture of the wreck.'),
 colour='#235e69',
 country='This file is about a shipwreck off the coast of what is now Western Australia, and about the records that remain. People died when the ship struck, and many more were killed later, before help came. This file does not describe those deaths.',
 opener=['Monday morning, 4 June 1629.',
   'The journal of the commandeur, Francisco Pelsaert, opens on the reef of Frederick Houtman, on the second day of Whitsuntide, under a bright, clear, full moon.',
   'The Western Australian Museum’s account of that night says a white gleam of breaking surf was seen, and that the skipper took it for moonlight.',
   'Then the ship struck.'],
 agreed=['The Batavia was a ship of the Dutch East India Company (VOC). It left Texel, in the Netherlands, in late October 1628, bound for Batavia (now Jakarta).',
   'In the early hours of 4 June 1629 it struck a reef in the Houtman Abrolhos. Most people reached low islands nearby.',
   'Pelsaert, the skipper Ariaen Jacobsz and others left in the longboat to find water, then help. Pelsaert came back in the Sardam in mid-September 1629.',
   'While they were away, a mutiny was led by the under-merchant Jeronimus Cornelisz. A group of soldiers led by Wiebbe Hayes found water on a higher island and held out.',
   'Pelsaert’s handwritten journal survives in the Dutch National Archives. The wreck was found in 1963; objects from it are in the Western Australian Museum.'],
 evidence=[
  dict(kind='Handwritten journal · 1629', title='Pelsaert’s journal',
       cite='Nationaal Archief, The Hague, VOC archive 1.04.02, inventory no. 1098. An English translation by Marit van Huystee was published by the Western Australian Maritime Museum (Report No. 136, 1994).',
       quote=None, summary='The main written record of the wreck, the longboat voyage, the rescue and the trials. For the weeks of the mutiny Pelsaert was not on the islands; he wrote those down afterwards, from questions, confessions and other people’s memories.',
       look='How should we read a record of days the writer did not see?',
       links=[('Nationaal Archief inventory','https://www.nationaalarchief.nl/onderzoeken/archief/1.04.02/invnr/1098'),('English translation (PDF, WA Museum)','https://museum.wa.gov.au/maritime-archaeology-db/sites/default/files/no._136_pelsaertjournal.pdf')]),
  dict(kind='Museum interpretation', title='A museum on Pelsaert’s point of view',
       cite='Western Australian Museum, Batavia (e-book, 2022).',
       quote='Pelsaert was anxious to show that the wreck and aftermath were not his fault. As personal journals, they reflect his subjectivity and bias.',
       look='The museum still calls the journals the primary accounts. Can a source be biased and still be the best we have?',
       links=[('Western Australian Museum','https://visit.museum.wa.gov.au/batavia-ebook-2022-web/Batavia_ENG_01-90.xhtml')]),
  dict(kind='Printed book · 1647', title='Ongeluckige Voyagie, van ’t schip Batavia',
       cite='Published in Amsterdam in 1647 from Pelsaert’s journal; described by the State Library of Western Australia.',
       quote=None, summary='A printed book, popular and reprinted, with engravings made in Europe years later. A printed book is not the same thing as the handwritten journal in the archives.',
       look='Why might a famous later book still be the wrong proof for a question about the original journal?',
       links=[('State Library of Western Australia','https://slwa.wa.gov.au/stories/wa-history/francisco-pelsaerts-journal')]),
  dict(kind='Two museums, two numbers', title='How many people, and how many died at the wreck?',
       cite='Western Australian Museum, “Batavia’s history, 1628–1963”; National Museum of Australia, Defining Moments: “Wreck of the Batavia”.',
       quote=None, summary='The Western Australian Museum says the ship left Texel on 29 October 1628 “transporting 341 people”. Careful museum work often gives about 322 aboard when it struck, and about 40 drowned at the wreck. The National Museum of Australia says there were “about 330 people aboard” and that “about 100 people died in the immediate aftermath of the collision”.',
       look='When two museums disagree, what should a case file do instead of picking the more dramatic number?',
       links=[('Western Australian Museum','https://visit.museum.wa.gov.au/batavias-history-1628-1963'),('National Museum of Australia','https://www.nma.gov.au/defining-moments/resources/wreck-of-the-batavia')]),
 ],
 witnesses=[
  dict(name='Francisco Pelsaert', role='Commandeur of the Batavia · wrote the journal · died 1630', img='silhouettes/pelsaert.png', cap='A silhouette, not a likeness. No contemporary portrait is known for this file.',
       said='His journal opens on the morning the ship struck. He records the wreck, the landing on the islands, the longboat voyage for help, and his return on the Sardam. He also wrote down what people told him later about the weeks he had missed.',
       src='Pelsaert’s journal, Nationaal Archief, VOC 1.04.02, inv. nr. 1098.'),
  dict(name='Wiebbe Hayes', role='A soldier on the Batavia · led the group that held the higher island', img='silhouettes/hayes.png', cap='A silhouette, not a likeness.',
       said='We have no long statement in Hayes’s own hand. Pelsaert wrote that Hayes and his soldiers found water and food on a higher island, held out against the mutineers, and captured Cornelisz. Pelsaert later made him a sergeant.',
       src='Known mainly through Pelsaert’s journal. No book of Hayes’s own is known to survive.'),
  dict(name='The wreck of the Batavia', role='Found on Morning Reef in 1963 · objects now in the Western Australian Museum', img='silhouettes/batavia-wreck.png', cap='A ship silhouette, not a picture of the wreck itself.',
       said='The objects do not speak in words. Timbers, a stone portico and coins show that the ship was real, what it carried, and that it broke on that reef. They cannot tell us what was said on the islands.',
       src='Raised by Western Australian Museum teams from the 1970s; on show at the Shipwrecks Museum, Fremantle.'),
 ],
 inquiry=dict(
  people=['Whose voice reaches us only through Pelsaert’s pen?','Who on the islands left no record at all?'],
  documents=['A handwritten journal, a printed book of 1647, and objects from the reef: what can each prove that the others cannot?'],
  silences=['The weeks of the mutiny, when no commandeur was there to write them down.','The ship’s muster rolls and pay registers, which would have named the crew, are among the papers later lost.',
            'Two men, Wouter Loos and Jan Pelgrom de Bye, were left on the mainland in November 1629. Their fate is unknown.'],
  unsettled=['About 40 drowned at the wreck, or about 100? 322, 330 or 341 aboard?','What is the difference between a page never written and a list written and later lost?']),
 decide=['The wreck happened, and the records prove that, but they cannot tell us every day.','Some lists of the people on the ship were lost later.','We can’t know, from this file, which gaps were never written and which were lost.'],
 sleuths=['Other Dutch marks on this coast: Dirk Hartog left a pewter plate at Cape Inscription in 1616; Willem de Vlamingh replaced it in 1697. What can a plate prove that a journal cannot?',
          'Why might “we can’t know what happened to them” be the most honest finding about the two men left on the mainland? Take this further only with guidance from the Aboriginal communities of the mid-west coast and AIATSIS.'],
 app=('Batavia: What can the records still not tell us?','https://robmac10.github.io/batavia-case/','qr/batavia-qr.png'),
 credits='Silhouettes drawn for the Reopen the Record apps. No contemporary portraits of Pelsaert or Hayes are known for this file.',
),
